#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 505pt), 54pt, 150.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 758.76pt, "Page 3 of 18 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 82.26pt, "photopolymerization-based manufacturing in space. Such predictions are, however, not straightforward as the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 96.776pt, "temperature is affected by a large number of parameters, including material properties, thermal boundary conditions, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 111.357pt, "excitation light propagation, and sample geometry. Furthermore, the ability to experimentally replicate on Earth the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 125.873pt, "weightless conditions under which technologies must operate in space (via parabolic flights, ballistic projectiles, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 140.454pt, "and drop towers) is limited to brief periods that are usually too short to simulate realistic fabrication processes.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 337.8pt, "Figure 1. Observations of heat induced defects during in-space manufacturing of photopolymer lenses. (A)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(506.513pt, 337.8pt, " An image of ")
  #line-at(53.99pt, 349.323pt, "astronaut Eytan Stibbe conducting the fluidic lens experiment aboard the International Space Station. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(455.693pt, 349.323pt, "(B)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(469.008pt, 349.323pt, " The astronaut injected ")
  #line-at(53.99pt, 360.846pt, "photopolymer resins into ring-shaped frames. In microgravity, the dominant surface tension, along with boundary conditions ")
  #line-at(53.98pt, 372.309pt, "and the injected volume, determine the lens’s steady-state topography. After a short settling period, the resin was cured with ")
  #line-at(53.98pt, 383.832pt, "UV light, resulting in a solid optical component within minutes. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.399pt, 383.832pt, "(C)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(322.305pt, 383.832pt, " During the experiment, the astronaut noticed that the lenses ")
  #line-at(54pt, 395.355pt, "made of TJ-3704A exhibited severe blistering on their surface. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(309.239pt, 395.355pt, "(D)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(323.157pt, 395.355pt, " A typical fluidic lens fabricated on Earth: a reference lens ")
  #line-at(53.99pt, 406.878pt, "fabricated on Earth, under neutral buoyancy conditions, shows smooth, defect-free surfaces. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 425.22pt, "In this work, we first confirmed our hypothesis using a set of parabolic flight experiments that reproduced the ")
  #line-at(54.011pt, 439.801pt, "observations on-board the ISS under controlled conditions and clearly linked microgravity, polymer temperatures, ")
  #line-at(54.011pt, 454.317pt, "and surface defects. Building on these results, we developed an experimentally validated model")
  #line-at(503.005pt, 454.317pt, "that predicts ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 468.898pt, "polymerization states and temperature profiles during photopolymerization-based fabrication. The model is based ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 483.414pt, "on the unsteady heat equation with material properties that vary in space and time as a function of local temperature ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 497.93pt, "and polymer phase state. Heat generation is expressed as a source term that originates from the photopolymerization ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 512.511pt, "reaction, and which depends on the local light intensity, modeled by Beer-Lambert absorption. The model applies ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 527.027pt, "to both terrestrial and microgravity environments by prescribing appropriate heat transfer boundary conditions and ")
  #line-at(54.033pt, 541.608pt, "is applicable to any photopolymer following characterization of the polymer’s thermal and optical properties. ")
  #line-at(54.033pt, 562.13pt, "We demonstrate the use of the model as a design tool for determining the conditions under which successful, defect-")
  #line-at(54.033pt, 576.711pt, "free, polymerization can be achieved in a given environment. Moreover, we show that given a specific ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 591.227pt, "manufacturing approach and geometry, the model can be used to determine the constraints for suitable material ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 605.742pt, "properties. Finally, we use the model to investigate polymer surface blistering observed during our fluidic lens ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 620.324pt, "fabrication on the ISS. The model reveals significant thermal buildup in microgravity due to the absence of ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 634.839pt, "convection and accurately captures our observations in photopolymerization of TJ-3704A and Norland optical ")
  #line-at(54.022pt, 649.421pt, "adhesives in space. ")
]

// arXiv source page 4
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 758.76pt, "Page 4 of 18 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 83.22pt, "2.")
  #line-at(72pt, 83.22pt, "Methods ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(53.7pt, 104.1pt, "2.1.")
  #line-at(72.004pt, 104.1pt, "Investigation of photopolymerization in microgravity ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54.007pt, 124.677pt, "We hypothesize that the thermal defects observed in our ISS experiment can be attributed to the absence of natural ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 139.192pt, "convection in microgravity, which led to a significant reduction in heat dissipation. To test this hypothesis, we ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 153.773pt, "conducted a series of parabolic flight experiments in which photopolymer samples were UV-cured while recording ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 168.289pt, "their thermal response. A parabolic flight consists of repeated free-fall maneuvers providing ~15 sec intervals of ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 182.87pt, "microgravity, as well as ‘straight and level’ flight in proper acceleration of 1g. By systematically repeating the ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 197.386pt, "experiments in both 1g and microgravity conditions, and applying either natural or forced convection cooling, we ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 211.967pt, "were able to isolate the respective roles of gravity and convective heat transfer, serving as the basis for our analytical ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 226.483pt, "model. Additionally, these experiments allowed us to reproduce and analyze the polymer behavior previously ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 240.999pt, "observed in our ISS study.  ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 261.575pt, "We conducted the experiments aboard two consecutive parabolic flights. As shown in Figure 2, each flight carried ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 276.091pt, "16 custom, airtight desiccators, each containing two to four 30 mm wide polystyrene petri dishes, each filled with ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 290.672pt, "photopolymer resin up to a predetermined height (1-5 mm) – dimensions that correspond to the lenses produced ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 305.188pt, "during our ISS experiment. To prevent cross-contamination, each desiccator housed only a single polymer ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 319.769pt, "formulation. We tested all three photopolymers used in the ISS experiment: TJ-3704A, NOA61, and NOA63. The ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 334.285pt, "outer walls of the desiccators were lined with UV-resistant Kapton tape to shield them from ambient light. ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 348.866pt, "Throughout the flights, we initiated the photopolymerization of the samples within the desiccators (two desiccators ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 363.382pt, "at a time), via remotely controlled 365 nm LEDs mounted on the desiccator lids, operated at various pre-calibrated ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 377.897pt, "UV intensities. We embedded thermocouples at different depths within the polymer samples to measure the ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 392.479pt, "temperature evolution. To regulate airflow, we used voltage-controlled fans mounted inside the chambers to produce ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 406.994pt, "laminar flow at approximately 4 m/s parallel to the polymer's free surface. ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 427.571pt, "To ensure proper control experiments, we activated 6 of the 16 desiccators (per flight) during the 1g ‘straight and ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 442.086pt, "level’ flight segments. Turning the fans off resulted in natural convection under 1g conditions and no convection in ")
  #line-at(53.986pt, 456.668pt, "0g. Activating the fans established consistent forced convection in both gravity regimes. We logged the ")
  #line-at(53.986pt, 471.183pt, "thermocouple measurements as well as the UV-LED and fan control-signals at 25 Hz, and time-synchronized them ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 485.699pt, "with inertial data gathered by an Arduino-based inertial measurement unit (IMU) which was mounted to the ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 500.28pt, "experimental setup. ")
  #line-at(53.985pt, 520.802pt, "We used remotely activated GoPro cameras - equipped with custom 35 mm macro-lenses and mounted inside 8 of ")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 535.383pt, "the 16 desiccators - to capture the photopolymerization process and the emergence of thermal deformations. As ")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 549.899pt, "shown in Figure 2C, these recordings documented visible evidence of thermal effects such as boiling, bubbling, and ")
  #line-at(53.974pt, 564.48pt, "evaporation as well as the formation of surface blisters similar to those previously observed in our ISS experiments. ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 585.002pt, "Figure 2D presents a blister density count (normalized by the largest value of all samples) as obtained from the ")
  #line-at(53.996pt, 599.584pt, "polymerized TJ-3704A samples, as a function of the maximum temperature recorded in the sample during the ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 614.099pt, "experiments. The three datasets presented correspond to natural convection during the ‘straight and level’ flight (1g, ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 628.615pt, "fan off), natural convection during microgravity (0g, fan off), and forced convection during microgravity (0g, fan ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 643.196pt, "on). Although significant defects were observed in some samples in 1g conditions, the absence of gravity ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 657.712pt, "exacerbated the issue. Samples polymerized in microgravity exhibited both higher average temperatures and ")
  #line-at(54.007pt, 672.293pt, "approximately double the blister count compared to those polymerized in 1g, under otherwise identical experimental ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 686.809pt, "conditions. Introducing fan-induced forced convection had a pronounced cooling effect, even at the modest airflow ")
  #line-at(54.018pt, 701.39pt, "velocities tested. Average sample temperatures dropped by approximately 20 °C, and thermal defects were virtually ")
  #line-at(54.029pt, 715.906pt, "non-existent in the fan-cooled samples. Interestingly, measured temperatures did not exceed ~ 105 °C regardless of ")
]

// arXiv source page 5
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 758.76pt, "Page 5 of 18 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 82.26pt, "experimental parameters, which leads us to suspect that substantial heat-loss through devolatilization within the ")
  #line-at(54.011pt, 96.842pt, "polymer occurs above a critical temperature")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(248.28pt, 92.82pt, "8")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(251.76pt, 96.84pt, ".  This is supported by the visual observations in the video recordings ")
  #line-at(53.999pt, 112.256pt, "(see Supplementary Video4). ")
  #line-at(53.999pt, 132.778pt, "These experimental findings support our hypothesis that the effect of gravity on photopolymerization is primarily ")
  #line-at(53.999pt, 147.359pt, "through the suppression of natural convective heat transfer. Although fan-induced airflow effectively mitigated ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 161.875pt, "thermal buildup in our relatively thin samples, it is not a viable solution for thicker and larger structures and is ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 176.456pt, "entirely impractical for construction in open space, where no atmosphere exists. In addition, the induced airflow ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 190.971pt, "across the liquid-air interface can distort the geometry of cured components, making fan cooling unsuitable for ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 205.487pt, "objects where precision is of high importance, such as optics. However, it is likely that other parameters, such as ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 220.068pt, "polymer thickness, UV intensity, and material properties, can be adjusted to enable defect-free fabrication in space. ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 234.584pt, "As the combined influence of these parameters on thermal behavior is complex and not easily predictable, we now ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 249.165pt, "turn to the development of a model which incorporates these parameters and is capable of accurately forecasting ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 263.681pt, "temperature evolution during fabrication. ")
]

