  #line-at(53.98pt, 595.837pt, "of fumes (see Supplementary Video 4). As the appearance of the fumes coincides with the peak temperature, we suspect ")
  #line-at(53.97pt, 607.36pt, "substantial heat loss through evaporation. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(228.388pt, 607.36pt, "(D)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(242.306pt, 607.36pt, " Normalized surface blister density as a function of TJ-3704A samples peak ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 618.823pt, "temperature and convective heat flow: blister density increases with peak temperature and is significantly more pronounced in ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 630.346pt, "0g without convection. The three datasets presented correspond to natural convection during ‘straight and level’ flight (1g, fan ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 641.869pt, "off), natural convection during microgravity (0g, fan off), and forced convection during microgravity (0g, fan on). While some ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 653.332pt, "defects are observed in 1g conditions, microgravity exacerbates the issue greatly. Samples polymerized in microgravity ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 664.855pt, "exhibited both higher average temperatures and approximately double the blister count compared to those polymerized in 1g, ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 676.378pt, "under otherwise identical experimental conditions. Fan-induced forced convection had a pronounced cooling effect, even at the ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 687.841pt, "modest airflow velocities tested (~4 m/s). Average sample temperatures dropped by approximately 20 °C, and thermal defects ")
  #line-at(53.95pt, 699.364pt, "were virtually non-existent in the fan-cooled samples. ")
]

