  #line-at(54.02pt, 251.455pt, "away from the dish. ")
  #line-at(54.02pt, 271.976pt, "Figure 6B compares the experimental temperature measurements with the corresponding model predictions, ")
  #line-at(54.02pt, 286.492pt, "obtained without any fitting parameters. Overall, the model demonstrates strong agreement with the experiments, ")
  #line-at(54.02pt, 301.073pt, "successfully capturing the temporal evolution of temperature at multiple depths within the polymer both during the ")
  #line-at(54.009pt, 315.589pt, "active heat generation at early times and under cool-down at long times. At shallow depths near the polymer–air ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 330.17pt, "interface (yellow curve, z = 1.7 mm), the measured thermal behavior differs from that observed deeper in the ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 344.686pt, "material. While the model accurately predicts the initial heat-up and the long-time cooling of this near-surface ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 359.267pt, "region, it overestimates the peak temperature during the intermediate times when the surface reaches its maximum ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 373.783pt, "temperature. This discrepancy is localized to the regions closest to the free surface and does not appear at greater ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 388.364pt, "depths. Given that the TJ-3704A resin formulation contains between 5 to 15 wt.% of HPMA (hydroxypropyl ")
  #line-at(54.031pt, 402.88pt, "methacrylate), a component known to be volatile at the measured temperature range")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(451.68pt, 398.4pt, "22")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(458.7pt, 402.9pt, ", we hypothesize that ")
  #line-at(53.999pt, 417.416pt, "evaporative cooling, neglected in our model, is responsible for the reduced measured temperature at the interface. ")
  #line-at(54.01pt, 431.997pt, "This explanation is consistent with the substantial surface blistering observed on this sample and falls in line with ")
  #line-at(53.999pt, 446.513pt, "our parabolic-flight observations where excess temperatures correlated with the formation of blistering (Figure 2). ")
]

