#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 758.76pt, "Page 15 of 18 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 82.26pt, "The lenses produced in the ISS experiments had a characteristic thickness of 4 mm and were cured from both sides ")
  #line-at(54pt, 97.204pt, "simultaneously by 365 nm UV light with an intensity of 0.455 ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(334.616pt, 97.204pt, "mW/cm")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(373.32pt, 93.18pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(378.36pt, 97.2pt, ". During their curing, the polymerization ")
  #line-at(54pt, 111.902pt, "chamber was closed, inhibiting any airflow within the chamber. This, together with the absence of gravity, implies ")
  #line-at(54pt, 126.418pt, "that no heat could be lost from the lens through convection. We thus modelled heat dissipation out of the lens ")
  #line-at(54pt, 140.999pt, "through pure conduction")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(163.44pt, 136.5pt, "23")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(170.46pt, 141pt, ", with a reference temperature of 23 °C at a distance of 50 mm from the surface of the ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 155.516pt, "lens. ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 176.037pt, "As shown in Figure 6B, our simulations indicate that the TJ-3704A lenses exceeded their critical temperature ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 190.619pt, "threshold (as found by the TGA) approximately one minute into curing, consistent with video footage of the ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 205.134pt, "experiment showing the escape of fumes from the curing chamber when it was opened after 4 min of curing (see ")
  #line-at(53.995pt, 219.716pt, "Supplementary Video 2), and with the severe surface blistering observed on the returned ISS samples (Figure 1C). ")
  #line-at(53.984pt, 234.231pt, "The model also shows that the NOA resins remained below their respective thresholds, which again matches the ")
  #line-at(53.984pt, 248.813pt, "experimental outcomes in which these lenses were defect-free. ")
  #line-at(53.984pt, 269.334pt, "The use of the model is, of course, not limited to post-experimental investigations and can be used as a design tool ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 283.916pt, "for future in-space manufacturing processes. With the model, we can simulate variations of experimental conditions ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 298.431pt, "such as polymer thickness and light intensity variation over time. We can also investigate desired material properties ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 312.947pt, "and their role in thermal regulation as well as thermal control through external conditions such as forced convection ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 327.528pt, "or reduced environmental temperatures. As an example, we used the model to quantify what magnitude of forced ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 342.044pt, "convection would be needed to avoid the onset of thermal defects under otherwise identical experimental conditions. ")
  #line-at(53.973pt, 356.625pt, "The black curve in Figure 6C presents the case where the polymer-air interface is subjected to a Newton cooling ")
  #line-at(53.984pt, 371.569pt, "law boundary condition with a forced heat transfer coefficient of 50 ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(354.408pt, 371.569pt, "W/(m")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(383.64pt, 367.56pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(388.74pt, 371.58pt, "K)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(400.258pt, 371.58pt, ". This translates to a laminar airflow ")
  #line-at(54.004pt, 386.216pt, "of approximately 5 m/s, which can be easily achieved with a small fan. The simulation indicates that such forced ")
  #line-at(54.004pt, 400.798pt, "convection would be sufficient to prevent the overheating of the TJ-3704A samples.")
  #line-at(425.52pt, 400.8pt, "This result is supported by our ")
  #line-at(54.001pt, 415.316pt, "experimental findings from the parabolic flight tests. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 436.86pt, "4.")
  #line-at(72pt, 436.86pt, "Conclusions and Outlook ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 457.74pt, "Future long-duration space missions, such as crewed Mars expeditions, will require in-situ, on-demand ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 472.321pt, "manufacturing capabilities. Photopolymer-based manufacturing processes are promising as they inherently require ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 486.837pt, "little energy and simple infrastructure, and as liquid-resin cargos are very volume-efficient. However, heat released ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 501.418pt, "during rapid photopolymerization can lead to mechanical defects if left unmanaged. Regulating this heat is generally ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 515.934pt, "challenging due to the polymer’s low thermal conductivity and particularly challenging in microgravity, where ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 530.45pt, "natural convection does not occur.")
  #line-at(54pt, 551.04pt, "Photopolymer resins are complex mixtures of monomers, photoinitiators, diluents, inhibitors, and other additives. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 565.556pt, "As a result, predicting their thermal behavior during polymerization is challenging, and usually requires detailed ")
  #line-at(54pt, 580.137pt, "knowledge of proprietary formulations. In this work, we demonstrated an experimental characterization method that ")
  #line-at(54pt, 594.653pt, "bypasses the need for such prior knowledge. Specifically, we propose measuring both the rate of heat generation ")
  #line-at(54pt, 609.234pt, "and the total heat released through UV-DSC measurements. These measurements reveal that material response is ")
  #line-at(54pt, 623.75pt, "highly sensitive to UV intensity, implying that accurate process modeling must account for spatial variations in light ")
  #line-at(54pt, 638.331pt, "distribution within the sample. This challenge is further compounded by the strong absorption of UV light in such ")
  #line-at(54pt, 652.847pt, "polymers, which results in intensity gradients in the material. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 673.368pt, "We developed a predictive thermal model and validated it using laboratory and parabolic flight data.  By integrating ")
  #line-at(54pt, 687.95pt, "heat transfer, light absorption, and evolving material properties, the model predicts temperature evolution during ")
  #line-at(54pt, 702.465pt, "curing under various environmental conditions, including microgravity. Simulations based on the model can serve ")
  #line-at(54pt, 717.047pt, "as a practical tool for defect-free in-space manufacturing, supporting the transition from Earth-based to in-space ")
]

// arXiv source page 16
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(54pt, 758.76pt, "Page 16 of 18 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 82.26pt, "methods. For instance, when applied to our ISS experiments, it accurately explained TJ-3704A resin failures and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 96.776pt, "demonstrated the effectiveness of forced convection as a strategy to prevent overheating in this particular process.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 117.352pt, "The model does not include heat loss through evaporation or boiling. While including such effects could extend the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 131.868pt, "validity of the model, they are not strictly necessary as the desired work point of practical applications is one at ")
  #line-at(54pt, 146.449pt, "which temperatures remain below their critical threshold and defects associated with these phase changes are ")
  #line-at(54pt, 160.965pt, "avoided. If additional accuracy is desired at borderline temperatures, such effects should be included.  ")
  #line-at(54pt, 181.541pt, "The solutions we presented are one-dimensional and assume uniform illumination with material absorption that ")
  #line-at(54pt, 196.057pt, "follows the Beer-Lambert law. Such modeling is directly applicable to photopolymerization fabrication methods ")
  #line-at(54pt, 210.638pt, "such as polymer casting, coating, or our Fluidic Shaping which utilize UV exposure over large areas to flood-cure ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 225.154pt, "elements. The model can be adapted for other methods such as DLP and SLA printing by considering a non-uniform ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 239.856pt, "intensity map at the boundary, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(188.692pt, 239.856pt, "𝐼𝐼(𝑥𝑥, 𝑦𝑦, 𝑧𝑧= 0, 𝑡𝑡)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(257.69pt, 239.856pt, " and by generalizing the heat equation (eq. 5) to include in-plane heat ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 254.558pt, "conduction. Methods such as VAM, which modulate light intensity through interference, would have to include this ")
  #line-at(53.989pt, 269.074pt, "intensity modulation in the source term of the thermal model. Despite being one-dimensional, the model is useful ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 283.59pt, "for designing the allowable working conditions for avoiding defects or boiling in three-dimensional objects being ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 298.171pt, "polymerized. The case we have focused on is that of lenses, which have a very regular axisymmetric shape. ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 312.687pt, "However, we expect the model to be equally relevant for more general shapes, as long as their dimensions are ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 327.268pt, "significantly larger than the absorption depth. For such objects, the polymerization can be considered to occur on a ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 341.784pt, "thin outer ‘shell’ of the object, and thus still be represented well by the 1D model. However, if one desired an ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 356.365pt, "accurate prediction of the temperature distribution in time and space, both the heat and UV propagation models ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 370.881pt, "should be expanded to 3D. In such a 3D heat model, the heat conduction operator will naturally take its operator ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 385.462pt, "form, but the source term in eq. 5 will remain unchanged.  ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 405.984pt, "In summary, our findings emphasize that effective thermal management is essential for reliable in-space ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 420.499pt, "photopolymer manufacturing. The validated model provides a practical design tool needed to adapt Earth-based ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 435.081pt, "photopolymerization processes for application in space, and even more so for the development of microgravity-")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 449.596pt, "based methods that cannot be tested on Earth. We believe that such models and simulations are essential for paving ")
  #line-at(53.978pt, 464.178pt, "the way toward scalable and dependable in-space manufacturing for future long-duration missions.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
